  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226505/assets/db1b39e3f00fd6bb64ed738fb12213f4775d2bd7d098dfa9d0efec2192d7a64f.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m∠1.$",
  "worked_solution": "Consider $∠2$ and $∠3$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226505/assets/a775b7c9ce63aa5b9f8c03fb91e7018d2f1edde5a2a2acdb099ae5ba53a874e4.png)\n\nNote that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${75}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2={75}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠3$ and the angle whose measure is ${50}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠3={50}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226505/assets/01462502a4fbe0b25e489cc611200beb47cd926b8a055b69b7279aa0f301850b.png)\n\nThe angles $∠1$, $∠2,$ and $∠3$ combine to give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2+m∠3 & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{75}^{∘}+{50}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{125}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1 & ={55}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
